<p>GTA VI is not part of the Game Pass library. You buy it separately, at $79.99 or $99.99, in the Xbox store of your account's region.</p>

<h2>Playing without a current console</h2>
<p><strong>Xbox One, by cloud.</strong> Xbox Cloud Gaming offers GTA VI on Xbox One and Series X and S consoles, and on nothing else at launch: no phone, browser, television or PC. You must own the game and have cloud hours from a Game Pass plan (5, 10 or 15 a month, depending on the tier) or buy hours. The picture follows the Series S settings. Nepal has not been an officially supported Xbox country, there is no data centre here, and delay across the border is noticeable in driving and shooting. Try any cloud game before you count on it.</p>
<p><strong>Phones, computers and handhelds, by Remote Play.</strong> This sends the picture from a console you own. A PS5 can stream to Android, iPhone, Windows, Mac, some Android televisions and the PlayStation Portal. A Series X or S can stream to Android, iPhone, a Windows computer, a Windows handheld such as the <a href="/products/asus-rog-xbox-ally">ROG Xbox Ally</a>, and televisions on Microsoft's list. Over good home Wi-Fi it works well, and it needs no subscription. It still needs the console.</p>

<h2>What about a gaming PC?</h2>
<p>No PC version has been announced. Rockstar's habit is to release on consoles first: GTA V reached PC about nineteen months later and Red Dead Redemption 2 about a year later. Industry reports mention 2027, and nothing is official. If you are building a PC anyway, aim for a graphics card with at least 12GB of video memory and a modern eight-core processor, and treat GTA VI as a bonus. Our <a href="/custom-pc-build">PC builder</a> and <a href="/news/nvidia-rtx-50-series-blackwell-gpus-nepal-guide">RTX 50 guide</a> can help.</p>
<p>Files that claim to be a PC version today are stolen or fake. They carry real risk for your computer and your accounts.</p>

<h2>Storage: 212GB is a third of a small console</h2>
<p>Both platform holders ask for about 212GB of free space. Updates need room on top, so plan for 250GB. On a PS5 Digital you will keep three other large games beside it. Adding an NVMe drive is the cure: choose PCIe 4.0, with a heatsink, rated above 5,500MB per second. One terabyte is enough for most. On Xbox the expansion card is a special type and costs more, and a cheap external drive can store games but not run this one.</p>

